            <ArticleTitle>Risk factor panels associated with hypertension in obstructive sleep apnea patients with different body mass indexes.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>382-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jash.2015.01.015</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1933-1711(15)00042-X</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Although hypertension (HTN), obstructive sleep apnea (OSA), and obesity frequently co-occur, the precise role of obesity in this interrelationship is not completely understood. A total of 727 OSA patients were assigned to body mass index (BMI) &lt;25 (27.6%; n = 201), 25≤ BMI &lt;29.99 (53.4%; n = 388), and BMI ≥30 (19%; n = 138). HTN risk factors in each group were evaluated. A total of 244 (33.6%) patients exhibited co-morbid HTN, of whom 20.5% (50/244), 52.9% (129/244), and 26.6% (65/244) were distributed between the BMI &lt;25, 25≤ BMI &lt;29.99, and BMI ≥30 groups, respectively. Multiple logistic regression indicated that age, male gender, triglycerides (TG), low-density lipoprotein cholesterol (LDL-C), and apnea-hypopnea index (AHI) scores were HTN risk factors for the BMI&lt;25 group. In the 25≤ BMI &lt;29.99 group, risk factors were age, BMI, diabetes, and AHI. Finally, in the BMI ≥30 group, risk factors were age, diabetes, TG, LDL-C and AHI. These results demonstrate that different risk factor panels were associated with HTN in OSA patients with different BMIs.</AbstractText>
